\section*{Claim}
The paper's outputs form an instrumental chain with three distinct certification levels:
\[
\text{finite-}N\text{ trace closure}\Longrightarrow \text{convex }SU(2)\text{ feasible set}\Longrightarrow \text{certified plaquette intervals}\Longrightarrow \text{finite-loop Creutz estimates}.
\]
Only the first three arrows are certification-preserving without extra physical assumptions.
\section*{Premises}
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{(P1)}&\quad \text{Trace identities reduce generated }U(N)\text{ and }SU(N)\text{ loop equations to retained trace variables}.\\
\text{(P2)}&\quad \text{For }SU(2),\text{ the retained variables are single-trace Wilson-loop expectations }W(C).\\
\text{(P3)}&\quad \text{Loop equations are linear and positivity constraints are positive semidefinite affine matrix constraints}.\\
\text{(P4)}&\quad \text{Plaquette bounds are obtained by minimizing and maximizing }u_P\text{ over the same feasible set}.\\
\text{(P5)}&\quad \text{Creutz estimates are computed from finite rectangular Wilson-loop values}.\\
\text{(P6)}&\quad \text{A finite-rectangle Creutz value equals the physical string tension only after residual or large-loop control}.
\end{aligned}
\]
\section*{Derivation}
By (P1), the algebraic trace-reduction step supplies a closed finite list of retained Wilson-loop variables for the chosen finite-$N$ system. By (P2), the $SU(2)$ specialization removes all generated double-trace variables, so the retained numerical hierarchy can be formulated using single-trace variables alone. Combining (P2) with (P3) gives a convex feasible set of the form
\begin{align}
\mathcal F&=\{w:Aw=b,\ M_r(w)\succeq0\text{ for all }r\}.
\end{align}
By (P4), if the exact projected vector is feasible, then the plaquette value lies between the minimum and maximum of $u_P$ over $\mathcal F$. Hence plaquette intervals are certified consequences of the finite relaxation.

By contrast, (P5) gives a number extracted from retained rectangles, but (P6) states that the step from this finite rectangle to the physical string tension requires an additional assumption about residual terms or a large-loop limit. Therefore it must be reported as a qualitative finite-loop estimate rather than as a certified infinite-area bound.
\section*{Final Result}
\[
\boxed{\text{Closure and plaquette intervals are certified within the SDP relaxation; string tension is a finite-loop estimate unless residual control is added.}}
\]
\hfill$\blacksquare$
